=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router6
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** The iptables/nftables FORWARD chain on router6, specifically rules applied to inbound traffic on interface eth2 (the WAN-side link connecting router6 to router3 over the 10.50.1.0/24 segment).

**Mechanism:** Router6 acts as the default gateway for two subnets: 172.18.50.0/24 (eth0, serving laptop4/laptop5 via mswitch2 VLAN50) and 172.18.51.0/24 (eth1, serving pc15–pc18 via mswitch2 VLAN51). All outbound packets from these hosts leave router6 on eth2 toward router3 — the FORWARD chain does not block this direction. However, the return traffic (replies from 172.16.10.x, 192.168.200.x, etc.) re-enters router6 on eth2 and must be forwarded out through eth0 or eth1. The FORWARD chain on router6 contains blanket DROP rules matching `iifname "eth2"` for ICMP, TCP, and UDP — meaning every reply packet is silently dropped before it can reach the hosts behind router6. The packet counters confirm 22 ICMP packets (1,848 bytes) already dropped from our own probes.

**Localization:** router6, iptables/nftables FORWARD chain, rules targeting `in=eth2` (physical interface connecting to router3/rest-of-network). The sole exception is an ACCEPT for TCP port 80 from 172.16.10.0/24 destined to 172.18.51.1, which is far too narrow to restore general connectivity.

**Root cause:** Misconfigured stateless ACL rules on router6 block all forwarded traffic arriving from the upstream router (eth2), severing return-path reachability for the entire 172.18.50.x/172.18.51.x zone.

**Fix:** Remove (or replace with stateful `conntrack --ctstate ESTABLISHED,RELATED` ACCEPT rules) the three DROP rules in router6's FORWARD chain that match `iifname "eth2"`:
```
iptables -D FORWARD -i eth2 -p tcp -j DROP
iptables -D FORWARD -i eth2 -p udp -j DROP
iptables -D FORWARD -i eth2 -p icmp -j DROP
```
=== END ===